WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.940
Alternating voltage changes magnitude and sign with time.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.937
Our starting model is a sinusoid: voltage equals peak voltage V zero times

00:00:11.937 --> 00:00:12.220
sine

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.080
of omega t.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.645
Peak voltage is the largest positive value; the negative extreme is minus that

00:00:18.645 --> 00:00:18.940
peak.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.460
Omega is angular frequency in radians per second.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:28.200
Ordinary frequency f counts cycles per second, and omega equals two pi f.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.320
The period is one over f.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.440
For fifty hertz, a cycle lasts zero point zero two second.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.600
One quarter of a cycle lasts five milliseconds.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.460
Starting with sine at zero, the voltage reaches its positive peak after that

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.380
first quarter cycle.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.635
Suppose peak voltage is one hundred square root of two volts, about one

00:00:47.635 --> 00:00:47.980
hundred

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.160
forty one point four.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.740
That is not the same as a one hundred volt heating equivalent supply, although

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.020
we will connect them soon.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.262
The average voltage over a full cycle of a zero offset sinusoid is zero

00:01:00.262 --> 00:01:00.720
because

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.420
positive and negative parts cancel.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.400
Yet a resistor connected to it heats up.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.600
How can zero average voltage still transfer energy?

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.201
The power depends on the product of voltage and current, not simply the

00:01:13.201 --> 00:01:13.580
average

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:13.940
voltage.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.140
Keep instantaneous value, peak, cycle average and RMS in separate mental

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:19.420
drawers.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.880
A JEE question may switch between them in one line.

00:01:23.112 --> 00:01:25.032
Let's build RMS carefully.

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:27.792
The letters stand for root mean square.

00:01:28.752 --> 00:01:33.172
Square the changing voltage, average that square over a cycle, then take the

00:01:33.172 --> 00:01:33.552
square root.

00:01:34.692 --> 00:01:38.352
Squaring stops positive and negative portions from cancelling.

00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:43.836
For a sinusoid, the average of sine squared is one half, so RMS voltage is

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:44.132
peak

00:01:44.132 --> 00:01:46.512
voltage divided by square root of two.

00:01:46.992 --> 00:01:49.632
The same relation holds for sinusoidal current.

00:01:50.412 --> 00:01:55.012
Our peak voltage of one hundred square root of two volts therefore has RMS

00:01:55.012 --> 00:01:56.692
value one hundred volts.

00:01:58.232 --> 00:02:03.412
Across a fifty ohm resistor, RMS current is two amperes, and average heating

00:02:03.412 --> 00:02:06.732
power is two squared times fifty, or two hundred watts.

00:02:07.412 --> 00:02:11.624
That is the same heating as a steady one hundred volt DC source across the

00:02:11.624 --> 00:02:11.852
same

00:02:11.852 --> 00:02:12.152
resistor.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:17.152
It does not mean the instantaneous AC voltage stays at one hundred volts.

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:19.952
Try a peak current of six amperes.

00:02:20.552 --> 00:02:25.372
Its RMS value is about four point two four amperes, not three and not six.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:29.812
RMS is also not the average of the absolute value; those are different

00:02:29.812 --> 00:02:30.432
operations.

00:02:31.372 --> 00:02:36.021
For a square wave or another waveform, return to the RMS definition rather

00:02:36.021 --> 00:02:36.272
than

00:02:36.272 --> 00:02:38.372
blindly dividing the peak by square root of two.

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:42.052
We use zero offset sinusoids throughout this class.

00:02:42.972 --> 00:02:47.012
Label peak and RMS values before substituting into any power or impedance

00:02:47.012 --> 00:02:47.372
formula.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.820
Start with the simplest AC element: an ideal resistor.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.820
At every instant, current is voltage divided by resistance.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.560
If voltage is sinusoidal, current has the same frequency and phase.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.620
Their peaks and zero crossings occur together.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.440
When voltage is negative, current is negative too, so their product is still

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:08.820
positive.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.620
Instantaneous power equals current squared times resistance.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.540
It rises and falls but never becomes negative in this ideal resistor.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.740
Energy is continually converted into heat.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.606
For RMS voltage one hundred volts and resistance fifty ohms, RMS current is

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:26.800
two

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:27.300
amperes.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.720
Peak current is two square root of two amperes, about two point eight three.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.560
Average power is two hundred watts.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.640
Do not multiply peak voltage by RMS current and call that average power.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:45.199
Either use RMS voltage times RMS current for this in phase resistor, or use

00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:45.400
one

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.020
half peak voltage times peak current.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.600
They give the same result.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.580
The waveform diagram shows normalized voltage and current, so equal drawing

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.840
heights do not mean volts and amperes are numerically equal.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.280
The useful information is timing.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.880
In a resistor only circuit, changing frequency does not change the ideal

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:04.420
resistance.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.760
Real components can have parasitic effects at high frequencies, but our

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.100
elementary lumped element model leaves those out.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.940
For an ideal inductor, voltage equals L times the derivative of current.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:20.400
Think about the derivative of a sine: it is a cosine, a quarter cycle ahead.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.380
So in sinusoidal steady state, voltage leads current by ninety degrees.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.900
Equivalently, current lags voltage.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.400
That phrase refers to timing, not to current being slower in the wire.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.777
The opposition magnitude is inductive reactance X L, equal to omega L,

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:38.300
measured

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:38.800
in ohms.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.400
At fixed L, higher frequency gives greater reactance because the current must

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.920
change faster during each cycle.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.273
Use omega equal to one thousand radians per second and inductance zero point

00:04:50.273 --> 00:04:51.180
zero five henry.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.340
Reactance is fifty ohms.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.820
With RMS voltage one hundred volts, RMS current is two amperes.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.844
The arithmetic looks like division by resistance, but the energy story

00:05:01.844 --> 00:05:02.240
differs.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.940
An ideal inductor stores energy during part of the cycle and returns it during

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:07.520
another part.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.880
Average dissipated power is zero.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.440
Instantaneous power need not be zero.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.300
A real coil includes resistance and losses, so its phase and power are not

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.200
exactly those of a pure ideal inductor.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.920
The ideal formula also describes sinusoidal steady state, not a switch on

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.860
transient with arbitrary initial current.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.060
Always name the model before using the ninety degree rule.

00:05:29.232 --> 00:05:33.432
For an ideal capacitor, current equals capacitance times the voltage

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:33.872
derivative.

00:05:34.812 --> 00:05:39.112
A sinusoidal voltage therefore gives current a quarter cycle ahead: current

00:05:39.112 --> 00:05:40.932
leads voltage by ninety degrees.

00:05:41.992 --> 00:05:44.772
The capacitive reactance is one divided by omega C.

00:05:45.852 --> 00:05:49.872
Take omega equal to one thousand radians per second and C equal to twenty

00:05:49.872 --> 00:05:50.512
microfarads.

00:05:51.592 --> 00:05:54.672
Convert that capacitance to twenty times ten to the minus six farad.

00:05:55.812 --> 00:05:57.032
Reactance is fifty ohms.

00:05:57.772 --> 00:06:02.732
With RMS voltage one hundred volts, RMS current is again two amperes.

00:06:03.432 --> 00:06:07.392
The same magnitude as our inductor example does not mean the same phase: the

00:06:07.392 --> 00:06:08.572
lead and lag are opposite.

00:06:10.112 --> 00:06:12.312
Raise frequency while keeping component values fixed.

00:06:13.432 --> 00:06:16.852
Inductive reactance increases, but capacitive reactance decreases.

00:06:17.772 --> 00:06:20.812
Double frequency and X L doubles while X C halves.

00:06:21.692 --> 00:06:26.012
An ideal capacitor stores and returns electric field energy, giving zero

00:06:26.012 --> 00:06:28.652
average dissipated power in sinusoidal steady state.

00:06:29.552 --> 00:06:33.072
It is not a fifty ohm heater simply because its reactance is fifty ohms.

00:06:33.952 --> 00:06:38.332
At zero frequency steady DC, an ideal capacitor carries no sustained current

00:06:38.332 --> 00:06:42.552
after charging; do not directly substitute zero into a formula without

00:06:42.552 --> 00:06:43.672
interpreting the limit.

00:06:44.412 --> 00:06:48.412
The resistor, inductor and capacitor now give us three timing patterns.

00:06:49.392 --> 00:06:52.872
Next we will combine them using phasors rather than adding voltage magnitudes

00:06:52.872 --> 00:06:54.432
as ordinary positive numbers.
